    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\n**Testing in CI/CD** should make the test pipeline reproducible, observable, parallel where useful, and explicit about quality gates and artifacts.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the observable quality signal, inputs/context, expected result, failure conditions, and cleanup. Use the smallest test level that provides enough confidence.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example makes the core logic visible and easy to explain line by line.\n\n```ts\ninterface Job {\n  name: string;\n  passed: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction releaseReady(\n  jobs: readonly Job[],\n): boolean {\n  for (let i = 0; i < jobs.length; i += 1) {\n    if (!jobs[i].passed) return false;\n  }\n\n  return jobs.length > 0;\n}\n\nconst ready = releaseReady([\n  { name: \"unit\", passed: true },\n  { name: \"integration\", passed: true },\n]);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collection/platform helpers when they communicate the same behavior more clearly.\n\n```ts\nconst jobs = [\n  { name: \"unit\", passed: true },\n  { name: \"integration\", passed: true },\n];\n\nconst ready =\n  jobs.length > 0 &&\n  jobs.every((job) => job.passed);\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nKeep pipelines reproducible, observable, parallel where useful, and explicit about gates.\n\nStep 6 — Test quality:\nKeep the check deterministic, isolated, diagnostic, and representative. Cover success, boundary, failure, accessibility, and cleanup where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why the check belongs at this level, what it deliberately does not cover, how flakiness is prevented, and how it fits the wider QA strategy.",
